{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_a1b912c2-54de-48d9-828e-ebb737a76a07","role":"user","created":1775348397,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_5a2c9cb81c4c4cf580b3f99d","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"| FO-2. - Computed interest charge and computed interest rate on interest-bearing public debt | 21 | LIABILITIES TO FOREIGNERS REPORTED BY NONBANKING BUSINESS ENTERPRISES IN THE UNITED STATES | LIABILITIES TO FOREIGNERS REPORTED BY NONBANKING BUSINESS ENTERPRISES IN THE UNITED STATES |\n| FD-3. - Interest-bearing public debt | 22 | CM-IV-1. - Total liabilities by type | 86 |\n| FD-5. - Interest-bearing securities issued by Government agencies | 24 | CM-IV-3. - Total liabilities by type of country | 88 |\n| FO-7. - Maturity distribution and average length of marketable interest-bearing public debt | 26 | CM-V-1. - Total claims by type | 89 |\n| PUBLIC DEBT OPERATIONS | PUBLIC DEBT OPERATIONS | CM-VI-1. - Foreign purchases and sales of long-term domestic securities | 92 |\n| PDO-1. - Maturity schedule of interest-bearing marketable public debt securities | 30 | CM-VI-2. - Foreign purchases and sales of long-term foreign securities | 92 |\n| PDO-2. - Offerings of bills | 31 | CM-VI-3. - Net foreign transactions in marketable U.S. Treasury bonds and notes by country | 93 |\n| PDO-3. - New money financing through regular weekly Treasury bills | 33 | CM-VI-4. - Estimated foreign holdings of marketable U.S. Treasury bonds and notes by country | 94 |\n| PDO-4. - Offerings of public marketable securities | 35 | CM-VI-5. - Net foreign transactions in long-term domestic bonds | 95 |\n| PDO-5. - Unmatured marketable securities issued at a premium or discount | 38 | CM-VI-6. - Net foreign transactions in domestic stocks by country | 96 |\n| PDO-6. - Allotments by investor classes on subscriptions for public marketable securities | 42 | CM-VI-7. - Net foreign transactions in long-term foreign bonds by country | 97 |\n| PDO-7. - Disposition of public marketable securities | 44 | CM-VI-8. - Net foreign transactions in foreign stocks by country | 98 |\n| OFS-2. - Estimated ownership of public debt securities | 54 | FRENCH FRANC POSITIONS | FRENCH FRANC POSITIONS |\n| TSO-2. - Interest-bearing marketable public debt securities by type and maturity distribution | 56 | FCP-III-3. - Consolidated monthly bank positions | 106 |\n| TSO-3. - Interest-bearing marketable public debt securities by issue | 56 | GERMAN MARK POSITIONS | GERMAN MARK POSITIONS |\n| MQ-1. - Treasury bills | 60 | ITALIAN LIRA POSITIONS | ITALIAN LIRA POSITIONS |\nmaturity\nOn February 10 the Treasury announced that it would auction $5,250 million of 2-year notes to refund $3,907 million of notes maturing February 28, 1982, and to raise $1,343 million new cash. The notes offered were Treasury Notes of Series P-1984, to be dated March 1, 1982, due February 29, 1984, with interest payable on August 31, 1982; February 28 and August 31, 1983; and February 29, 1984. A coupon rate of 15-1/8% was set after the determination as to which tenders were accepted on a yield auction basis.\nTenders were received at Federal Reserve banks and branches and at the Bureau of the Public Debt, Washington, D.C. Bearer and registered notes were authorized to be issued in denominations of $5,000, $10,000, $100,000, and $1,000,000.\nOn February 16 the Treasury announced that it would auction $3,250 million of 5-year 2-month notes to raise new cash. The notes offered were Treasury Notes of Series E-1987, to be dated March 3, 1982, due May 15, 1987, with interest payable on November 15 and May 15 in each year until maturity. A coupon rate of 14% was set after the determination as to which tenders were accepted on a yield auction basis.\nTenders were received at Federal Reserve banks and branches and at the Bureau of the Public Debt, Washington, D.C. Bearer and registered notes were authorized to be issued in denominations of $1,000, $5,000, $10,000, $100,000, and $1,000,000.\n52-Week Bills\nOn February 11 tenders were invited for approximately $5,250 million of 364-day Treasury bills to be dated February 25, 1982, and to mature February 25, 1983. The issue was to refund $5,265 million of 364-day bills maturing February 25. Tenders were opened on February 18. They totaled $10,507 million, of which $5,250 million was accepted, including $451 million of noncompetitive tenders from the public and $1,420 million of the bills issued at the average price to\nIssues of regular weekly Treasury bills in February totaled $39,800 million. Those issues were to refund bills maturing in the amount of $36,711 million, and to raise about $3,050 million new cash. In the 13-week series there were three issues of $5,000 million and one issue of $4,900 million. In the 26- week series there were three issues of $5,000 million and one issue of $4,900 million. Average rates for the new issues are shown in the following table.\nTreasury Bills (Other than Cash Management Bills) - General\nTreasury bills are sold on a discount basis under competitive and noncompetitive bidding, and at maturity their par amount is payable without interest. All series are issued entirely in book-entry form in a minimum amount of $10,000 and in any higher $5,000 multiple, on the records either of the Federal Reserve banks and branches, or of the Department of the Treasury.\nTenders are received at Federal Reserve banks and branches and from individuals at the Bureau of the Public Debt, Washington. Each tender must be for a minimum of $10,000. Tenders over $10,000 must be in multiples of $5,000. Payment for the full par amount applied for must accompany all tenders submitted for bills to be maintained on the book-entry records of the Department of the Treasury. A cash adjustment will be made for the difference between the par payment submitted and the actual issue price as determined in the auction. No deposit need accompany tenders from incorporated banks and trust companies and from responsible and recognized dealers in investment securities for bills to be maintained on the book-entry records of Federal Reserve banks and branches. A deposit of 2 percent of the par amount of the bills applied for must accompany tenders for such bills from others, unless an express guaranty of payment by an incorporated bank or trust company accompanies the tenders.\nNoncompetitive tenders (without stated price), usually for $500,000 or less from any one bidder, for each issue of 13-week, 26-week, and 52-week bills, are accepted in full at the weighted average price of accepted competitive bids for an issue.\nPayment for accepted tenders must he made or completed on the date of issue, in cash or other immediately available funds or in a like par amount of Treasury securities maturing on or before that date. Payment for 13-week, 26-week, and 52-week bills may not be made by credit in Treasury tax and loan accounts.\nNonmarketable Foreign Series Securities - Foreign Governments\n| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Budget and off-budget results > Net receipts 1/ > (1) | Budget and off-budget results > Net outlays 2/ > (2) | Budget and off-budget results > Budget surplus or deficit (1-2) > (3) | Budget and off-budget results > Off-budget surplus or deficit > (4) | Budget and off-budget results > Total surplus or deficit > (5) | Means of financing--transactions > Borrowing from the public--Federal securities > Public debt securities | Means of financing--transactions > Cash and monetary assets (deduct) > Agency securities | Means of financing--transactions > Cash and monetary assets (deduct) > Investments of Government accounts 3/ | Means of financing--transactions > Cash and monetary assets (deduct) > Total 6+7-8 | Means of financing--transactions > Cash and monetary assets (deduct) > U.S. Treasury operating cash | Means of financing--transactions > Cash and monetary assets (deduct) > Special drawing rights 4/ | Unnamed: 12_level_0 > Unnamed: 12_level_1 > Other |\n| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Means of financing—net transactions—Continued > Reserve position on the U.S. quota in the IMF > (13) | Means of financing—net transactions—Continued > Other > (14) | Means of financing—net transactions—Continued > Transactions not applied to year's surplus or deficit > (15) | Means of financing—net transactions—Continued > Total financing > (16) | Selected balances end of period > U.S. Treasury operating cash > (17) | Selected balances end of period > Public debt securities > (18) | Selected balances end of period > Agency securities > (19) | Selected balances end of period > Investments of Government accounts > (20) | Selected balances end of period > Held by the public (18+19-20) > (21) |\n| Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Justice Department > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Labor Department > Unnamed: 2_level_1 | State Department > Unnamed: 3_level_1 | Transportation Department > Unnamed: 4_level_1 | Treasury Department > Interest on the public debt | Treasury Department > Other | Environmental Protection Agency > Unnamed: 7_level_1 |\n| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Federal employer contributions to retirement funds > Health and Human Services > Federal old-age, disability, and hospital insurance | Federal employer contributions to retirement funds > Office of Personnel Management > Civil service retirement and disability fund | Federal employer contributions to retirement funds > Other > Unnamed: 3_level_2 | Federal employer contributions to retirement funds > Total > Unnamed: 4_level_2 | Interest credited to certain Government accounts > Defense Department > Soldiers' and Airmen's Home permanent fund | Interest credited to certain Government accounts > Health and Human Services > Federal old-age and survivors trust fund | Interest credited to certain Government accounts > Health and Human Services > Federal disability insurance trust fund | Interest credited to certain Government accounts > Health and Human Services > Federal hospital insurance trust fund | Unnamed: 9_level_0 > Health and Human Services > Federal supplementary medical insurance trust fund 2/ |\n3/ Includes adjustment of interest on public debt issues to convert to the accrual basis. Also, includes Indian tribal funds, judicial survivors annuity fund, foreign service retirement and disability fund, airport and airway trust fund, and other.\n| Interest | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan |\n| Classification (trust funds) > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Outlays of trust funds > (9) | Interfund transactions > Total 4/ (10) | Interfund transactions > Federal payments and contributions (11) | Interfund transactions > Federal employer contributions (12) | Interfund transactions > Interest and profits on investments in Federal securities (13) | Interfund transactions > Other (14) | Interfund transactions > (15) | Unified budget outlays attributable to trust funds > (16) | Excess of receipts, or outlays (-) > Unnamed: 9_level_1 |\nUnder authority of Public Law 95-147, the Treasury implemented a program on November 2, 1978, to invest a portion of its operating cash in obligations of depositaries maintaining tax and loan accounts. Under the Treasury tax and loan investment program, depositary financial institutions select the manner in which they will participate in the program. Depositaries that wish to retain funds deposited in their tax and loan accounts in interest-bearing obligations participate under the Note Option; depositaries that wish to remit the funds to the Treasury's account at Federal Reserve banks participate under the Remittance Option.\nDeposits to tax and loan accounts occur in the normal course of business under a uniform procedure applicable to all financial institutions whereby customers of financial institutions deposit with them tax payments and funds for the purchase of Government securities. In most cases the transaction involves merely the transfer of funds from a customer's account to the tax and loan account in the same financial institution. On occasions, to the extent authorized by the Treasury, financial institutions are permitted to deposit in these accounts proceeds from subscriptions to public debt securities entered for their own account as well as for the accounts of their customers.\n| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Treasury operating balance 1/ Available funds in demand accounts at: > Federal Reserve banks | Treasury operating balance 1/ Available funds in demand accounts at: > Treasury tax and loan note accounts | Treasury operating balance 1/ Available funds in demand accounts at: > Other depositaries 2/ | Total 3/ > Unnamed: 4_level_1 | Funds in process of collection through- > Federal Reserve banks | Funds in process of collection through- > Other depositaries 4/ | Time deposits and other interest-bearing demand accounts > Unnamed: 7_level_1 | Other demand accounts 5/ > Unnamed: 8_level_1 | Coin, currency, and coinage metal > Unnamed: 9_level_1 | Miscellaneous items > Unnamed: 10_level_1 | Gold balance 6/ > Unnamed: 11_level_1 | Balance in account of the U.S. Treasury > Unnamed: 12_level_1 |\n| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month > Received directly | Federal Reserve accounts 1/ > Credits 2/ > Received through remittance option tax and loan depositaries | Federal Reserve accounts 1/ > Credits 2/ > Marketable issues | Federal Reserve accounts 1/ > Withdrawals 3/ > Withdrawals 3/ | Tax and loan note accounts > Proceeds from sales of securities 4/ > Nonmarketable issues 5/ | Tax and loan note accounts > Proceeds from sales of securities 4/ > Total credits | Tax and loan note accounts > Taxes 6/ > Unnamed: 6_level_2 | Tax and loan note accounts > Taxes 6/ > Unnamed: 7_level_2 | Withdrawals (transfers to Federal Reserve accounts) > Withdrawals (transfers to Federal Reserve accounts) > Unnamed: 8_level_2 |\n| End of fiscal year or month > End of fiscal year or month | Amount outstanding > Total | Amount outstanding > Public debt securities | Amount outstanding > Agency securities | Government accounts > Total | Government accounts > Public debt securities | Securities held by: The public 1/ > Agency securities | Securities held by: The public 1/ > Total | Securities held by: The public 1/ > Public debt securities | Unnamed: 9_level_0 > Agency securities |\nTable FD-2. - Computed Interest Charge and Computed Interest Rate on Interest-Bearing Public Debt (Dollar amounts in millions)\n| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Total interest-bearing public debt 1/ > Unnamed: 1_level_1 > Unnamed: 1_level_2 | Computed annual interest charge 2/ > Unnamed: 2_level_1 > Unnamed: 2_level_2 | Total interest-bearing public debt 1/ > Unnamed: 3_level_1 > Unnamed: 3_level_2 | Computed annual interest rate > Total > Unnamed: 4_level_2 | Computed annual interest rate > Marketable issues > Treasury bills 3/ | Computed annual interest rate > Unnamed: 6_level_1 > Treasury notes | Computed annual interest rate > Unnamed: 7_level_1 > Treasury bonds | Nonmarketable > Other 4/ > Unnamed: 8_level_2 | Nonmarketable > Government account series > Unnamed: 9_level_2 |\nSource: Bureau of Government Financial Operations. Note: The computed annual interest charge represents the amount of interest that would be paid if each interest-bearing issue outstanding at the end of each month or year should remain outstanding for a year at the applicable annual rate of interest. The aggregate charge for all interest-bearing issues constitutes the total computed annual interest charge. The average annual rate is compute by dividing the computed annual interest charge for the total or for any group of issues by the corresponding principal\n3/ Included in debt outstanding at face amount, but discount value is used in computing annual interest charge and annual interest rate.\n4/ On United States savings bonds, the rate of maturity is applied against the amount outstanding.\nTable FD-3. - Interest-Bearing Public Debt\n| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Total interest-bearing public debt > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Marketable > Total | Marketable > Treasury bills | Marketable > Treasury notes | Marketable > Treasury bonds | Nonmarketable > Total | Nonmarketable > U.S. savings bonds |\nNonmarketable - Continued\nSource: Monthly Statement of the Public Debt of the United States. 1/ Consists of certificates of indebtedness sold to foreign governments for U.S. dollars. 2/ Consists of the dollar equivalent of Treasury certificates of indebtedness paid in designated foreign currencies.\nSource: Monthly Statement of the Public Debt of the United States.\nTable FD-5. - Interest-Bearing Securities Issued by Government Agencies\nMemorandum - Interest-bearing securities of non-Government agencies 2/\nas agency debt. These transactions were previously reported as off-budget negative outlays. 1/ Includes unredeemed matured securities outstanding on which interest has ceased. 2/ For details of securities outstanding, see Table TSO-5.\nTable FD-7. - Maturity Distribution and Average Length of Marketable Interest-Bearing Public Debt Held by Private Investors\n| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Amount outstanding privately held > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Maturity classes > Within 1 year | Maturity classes > 1 - 5 years | Maturity classes > 5 - 10 years | Maturity classes > 10 - 20 years | Maturity classes > 20 years and over | Average length 1/ > Unnamed: 7_level_1 |\nof the interest-bearing marketable public debt is computed on that part of the outstanding public debt privately held.\n| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Statutory debt limit > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Debt o